<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The neo palette for the widget pack, as COLOUR RESOURCES rather than as
  runtime values, and this file exists because of a bug rather than a preference.

  Observed 2026-08-16, emulator Pixel_10_Pro_XL / API 37: the palette used to be
  computed in Kotlin and resolved with a single-value ColorProvider. Switching
  the device to dark mode left every tile light, and it stayed light through a
  forced APPWIDGET_UPDATE. A RemoteViews is inflated LATER, by the launcher, so
  whichever single colour was baked in while building the tree is simply the
  wrong one by then, and the widget has no way to ask.

  A colour RESOURCE has no such problem: the launcher resolves it against its own
  configuration at inflate time, so values-night/widget_colors.xml below is
  picked up the instant the device switches, with no refresh and no code.
  Glance reaches it through ColorProvider(resId), which is stable public API.
  (androidx.glance.color.DayNightColorProvider would express this directly and is
  annotated RestrictTo, so it is not ours to use.)

  ── These values are DERIVED, not designed ──
  Every hex here is the output of WidgetPalette's own arithmetic, which is the
  same colorSchemeFor() the app's theme uses, so 4.1's rule that a skin must
  reach every material still holds. The formula, per skin and brightness:

      primary = colorSchemeFor(skin, isDark).primary
      ground  = neoGround.mix(primary, isDark ? 0.10 : 0.06).alpha(0.78)
                the alpha is WidgetPalette.WIDGET_PANEL_ALPHA, added 2026-08-24: a widget
                panel is TRANSLUCENT over the launcher wallpaper, as the prototype’s is.
                0.78 is measured, not chosen — 0.72 is the lowest alpha that still holds
                WCAG AA on onSurface and 3:1 on onSurfaceVariant against a pure black AND
                a pure white wallpaper. WidgetPanelContrastTest enforces the floor.
      onVar   = neoInk.alpha(isDark ? 0.62 : 0.60)
      accent  = primary.mute(isDark)          // saturation down, lightness clamped

  where neoGround is #EFF1F5 light / #0C1520 dark and neoInk is #16202B / #EAF0F6.
  WidgetPaletteResourceTest recomputes all sixteen values and fails if this file
  drifts from that arithmetic, so the derivation is enforced rather than trusted.

  Adding a third skin means adding four names here and four in values-night.
  4.1 already requires a skin to declare a luminance contract, not only a hue.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- Aurora — the default skin. Ocean blue, muted into neo. -->
    <color name="gp_widget_aurora_ground">#C7E1E8F2</color>
    <color name="gp_widget_aurora_on_surface">#FF16202B</color>
    <color name="gp_widget_aurora_on_surface_variant">#9916202B</color>
    <color name="gp_widget_aurora_accent">#FF325E9E</color>

    <!-- Blossom — sunset rose, the same transform applied. -->
    <color name="gp_widget_blossom_ground">#C7ECE5EC</color>
    <color name="gp_widget_blossom_on_surface">#FF16202B</color>
    <color name="gp_widget_blossom_on_surface_variant">#9916202B</color>
    <color name="gp_widget_blossom_accent">#FF9E4164</color>

</resources>
